1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bb5 Nd4 5. Ba4 Bc5 6. d3 Qe7 7. O-O c6 8.
Nxd4 Bxd4 9. Be3 Bb6 10. Qe2 O-O 11. Bxb6 axb6 12. f4 d6 13. f5 g6 14. g4 gxf5
15. gxf5 Kh8 16. Kh1 Rg8 17. Rg1 Bd7 18. Bb3 b5 19. Raf1 Raf8 20. Qf3 Qe8 21.
Ne2 ({The text move is given in primary sources (The Scotsman, 11 August 1906
and Manchester Guardian, 13 August 1906) but secondary sources such as Big/
Mega Database and chessgames.com give} 21. Rxg8+ Rxg8 22. h3 Qf8 23. Ne2 Qh6
24. Bxf7 Rg7 25. Be6 Be8 26. Ng1 Rg5 27. Qe3 $4 Rxg1+ 28. Qxg1 Qxh3+ {0-1})
21... Rxg1+ 22. Nxg1 ({One reason why secondary sources may have given 21
Rxg8+ as the text move instead of 21 Ne2 is that White might have been
expected to play the more natural} 22. Rxg1 {at this point. The text move
looks odd, especially since he follows it up with 23 h3, leaving the knight to
return whence it came. The Manchester Guardian columnist questions the move:
"why not RxR?"}) 22... Rg8 23. h3 Qf8 24. Ne2 Qh6 25. Bxf7 Rg7 26. Be6 Be8 27.
Ng1 Rg5 28. Qe3 $4 Rxg1+ 29. Qxg1 Qxh3+ {(The Scotsman's score ends here: "and
wins")} 30. Qh2 Qxf1+ {"and Black won" according to the Manchester Guardian}
0-1

